  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two polymer-based strategies for reducing membrane fouling. On the left, hydrophilic polymers such as poly(sulfobetaine) and poly(ethylene oxide) are shown as side chains attached to a hydrophobic backbone. These chains form a visible hydration layer around the surface, creating a water-rich barrier that discourages foulants from sticking. In the schematic below, particles carried by water flow appear to stay away from the membrane pores, illustrating “fouling resistance.”\n\nOn the right, polymers with low surface energy, including poly(dimethylsiloxane) and a fluorinated methacrylate, are used in a different way. Here the coating includes a low-surface-energy block together with a hydrophilic segment. Rather than mainly preventing initial attachment, this design seems to weaken adhesion so deposited material can be removed more easily by flowing water. The lower schematic shows particles being swept off the surface, representing “fouling release.”\n\nOverall, the image highlights a contrast between resisting attachment through hydration and promoting detachment through low surface energy, both applied to modify membrane pore surfaces and improve performance in flowing water.",
